The Story's Potential — Honestly

This is a genuine story with a real engine. The premise — a man who was conscious during his coma, who knows things he shouldn't know, whose senses have been rewired, whose fiancee caused his fall, whose best friend betrayed him, and whose coma doctor is now following him with a gun — that's a thriller. That's a novel. The elements are all here.
What it needs is not a different story. It needs the story that's already here, restructured and deepened using King's principles:

	•	Hold the secrets longer. Sally's guilt, Rashford's betrayal, Dr. Wembley's predation — these are reveals that should build through behavior, not be stated by the narrator
	•	Stay in Thomas's head. His altered senses are the story's unique voice. The world filtered through his "knowingness" is what makes this different from any other recovery story
	•	Trust the concrete detail. Your best lines are all physical and specific. More of those, fewer explanatory paragraphs
